From b5e84377bb7a1d0390410c5d73620b340f6a9df9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 11:48:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] rtc: pm8xxx: treat a missing UEFI offset as zero

With "qcom,uefi-rtc-info" the driver reads the RTC offset from the
RTCInfo UEFI variable and fails the probe when the variable does not
exist. U-Boot keeps the offset in RTCInfo and creates the variable on
the first GetTime() or SetTime() call, so a system booted without one
has no RTC:

  rtc-pm8xxx ...: probe with driver rtc-pm8xxx failed with error -2

A missing variable means the time was never adjusted. Use a zero offset;
set_time() creates the variable when the firmware lets it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
---
 drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
index e624f848c22b..399474ee95d8 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
@@ -109,6 +109,12 @@ static int pm8xxx_rtc_read_uefi_offset(struct pm8xxx_rtc *rtc_dd)
 				     &size, rtc_info);
 	efivar_unlock();
 
+	/* The firmware may create the variable only when the time is set. */
+	if (status == EFI_NOT_FOUND) {
+		rtc_dd->offset = 0;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	if (status != EFI_SUCCESS) {
 		dev_dbg(dev, "failed to read UEFI offset: %lu\n", status);
 		return efi_status_to_err(status);
-- 
